 "paper": {
  "tldr": "Soccer staff use most recovery strategies on match day and the day after, and perceived effectiveness matched frequency of use for only 5 of 15 strategies. Competing staff interests and limited resources are the main barriers.",
  "findings": "The three most important recovery objectives were alleviating muscle damage or fatigue, minimising injury risk, and performance optimisation. Most teams used active recovery (97%), structured recovery days (94%), extra rest days (94%), massage (92%), cold-water therapy (91%), carbohydrate provision (90%) and protein provision (85%). Associations between perceived effectiveness and frequency of use were significant for sleep medication (P < .001, Cramer's V 0.48), carbohydrate (P = .007, 0.60), protein (P = .007, 0.63), extra rest day (P < .001, 0.56) and structured recovery day (P = .049, 0.50), and all strategies were used most often on MD or MD+1."
