{
 "evidenceSummary": "A PRISMA systematic review of CINAHL, MEDLINE and SPORTDiscus, with reference-list searching, identifying 72 peer-reviewed studies of the validity and/or reliability of GNSS, local positioning and inertial devices for team sport movement (62 from the database search, 10 from reference lists). Quality scores on a modified Downs and Black checklist ranged from 64% to 100% (mean 86.4%). Validity and reliability depended on device brand, sampling rate, movement type and intensity: 10 Hz or higher GNSS was generally more accurate and reliable than 5 Hz or 1 Hz devices, LPS distance error was much larger with sub-optimal set-up, and LPS velocity and acceleration accuracy fell with repeated change of direction.",
 "paper": {
  "tldr": "Wearable GPS and tracking data are only as good as the specific device, sampling rate and movement being measured. Devices at 10 Hz or above were generally more accurate and reliable than 1 or 5 Hz units, and several metrics have been tested in only one or a few studies.",
  "findings": "72 studies were included: 384 records retrieved, 187 duplicates removed, 88 full texts assessed, 62 included plus 10 from reference lists. GNSS was generally valid for total distance in shuttle, continuous and team sport circuit movements, but 1 Hz and 5 Hz devices were less accurate for short straight-line and direction-change movements; GNSS was generally accurate for peak velocity in straight-line sprinting. Between-device reliability was better for 10 Hz and above, and 5 Hz devices showed poor reliability for accelerations above 3 m/s2. LPS was accurate for distance when set up correctly (bias 0.5 to 1.8%) but not with sub-optimal set-up (bias 15.0 to 29.5%), and its instantaneous velocity and acceleration errors rose with repeated change of direction. Intra-device reliability was poorly studied because identical movements cannot be repeated."
 },
 "verification": {
  "status": "CORRECTED",
  "changes": [
   "paper.findings: attributed the 15 to 30% LPS distance bias to both sub-optimal set-up and repeated change of direction -> the bias is for sub-optimal set-up only (\"bias = 15.0-29.5%... compared to 'optimal' set-up (bias = 0.5-1.8%)\"); LPS distance was \"not compromised by frequent change of direction\", while instantaneous velocity and acceleration were",
   "evidenceSummary: same LPS conflation clarified (distance vs velocity/acceleration)",
   "paper.tldr: 'many metrics have too few studies to trust' -> 'several metrics have been tested in only one or a few studies'"
  ],
  "residualConcerns": "Year 2020 comes from the '(c) Crown 2020' line and the 28 November 2020 acceptance date; no issue volume or year is printed in the text.",
  "spotCheckPriority": "high"
 }
}

