{
 "evidenceSummary": "A retrospective observational study of 36 patients admitted to an ICU after heat illness following high-intensity exercise (35 male, 1 female; mean age 22.1 years), split into a good-prognosis group (under 7 days, n=22) and a poor-prognosis group (7 days or more, n=14). Blood lactate fell significantly by 2 h and 6 h in the good-prognosis group, while in the poor-prognosis group it fell only by 6 h; at 2 h, lactate was 1.2 +/- 0.5 mmol/L (good) versus 4.2 +/- 1.2 mmol/L (poor). The 2-h lactate clearance rate was higher in the good-prognosis group (P < 0.05) and was significant in logistic regression (P = 0.041), while the 6-h rate was not (P = 0.966).",
 "screeningLimitations": "Single-centre retrospective study of 36 patients with 14 in the poor-prognosis group, so estimates are imprecise. The 45.5% clearance cut-off was applied in the same sample, its derivation is not described and it is not validated, and the sample is hospitalised patients rather than athletes sampled for performance or health monitoring.",
 "paper": {
  "tldr": "In hospitalised athletes with heat illness, lactate that stayed high and a slow 2-hour lactate clearance went with a poor prognosis (7 days or more). The 45.5% clearance cut-off has not been validated outside this sample.",
  "limitations": "Retrospective single-centre design with 36 patients (14 poor-prognosis), and no external validation of the 45.5% threshold, whose derivation is not described. Eleven of the 14 poor-prognosis patients reached hospital more than 24 h after symptom onset, which the authors cite as a possible confounder. The abstract reports R2 = 0.83 for the LCR model, but the results give 0.835 as the correlation between lactate and APACHE II, and no model fit is reported for LCR. The text gives inconsistent study periods (December 2019 to July 2024 versus January 2020 to June 2024) and both a consent waiver and written consent, does not define what the 7-day prognosis cut-off measures, and has no exercise or performance data.",
  "rtp": "This study cannot inform return-to-sport decisions. Its outcome is a 7-day prognosis split during hospital treatment, not return to play or competition."
 },
 "verification": {
  "status": "CORRECTED",
  "changes": [
   "paper.tldr and paper.rtp: described the outcome as length of hospital stay -> the text only gives '< 7 days' versus '>= 7 days' prognosis groups without defining the measure",
   "evidenceSummary: 'only lactate-clearance variable retained as a predictor' -> 2-h LCR P = 0.041 and 6-h LCR P = 0.966 (Table 3)",
   "screeningLimitations and paper.limitations: 'cut-off derived in the same sample' -> derivation not described; added inconsistent study dates and consent statements"
  ],
  "residualConcerns": "Clinically sensitive heat-illness prognosis paper. The text conflicts on study period and on consent, does not define the 7-day prognosis outcome, and the abstract R2 = 0.83 appears to be the lactate and APACHE II correlation.",
  "spotCheckPriority": "high"
 }
}

